Chapter: PciBridgeDeep
  grounds Device.Port
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 Multi-level descent. pci-scan-max-depth is 3 and pci-collect has always been
 written to recurse, but every bed has presented ONE bridge one level deep, so
 depth 2 and 3 have never executed anywhere. A recursion that is reachable and
 untravelled reads exactly like a tested walk. The ASUS presents 21 devices
 over four buses, and the first honest verdict on a real topology is still the
 board; this is the level the bed can add.

 pci-bridge-scan is the control and it must not move: -pci-bridge-deep is a
 separate flag precisely so the one-level counts keep their floor.

Section: Body
  count-on-bus : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  count-on-bus (devs) (i) (n) (bus) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else if (list-at devs i).pci-bus == bus then count-on-bus devs (i + 1) n bus (acc + 1)
   else count-on-bus devs (i + 1) n bus acc

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let scan = pci-scan-all
   in let n = scan.count
   in let b1 = count-on-bus (scan.devices) 0 n 1 0
   in let b2 = count-on-bus (scan.devices) 0 n 2 0
   in print-line-uni ("scan count=" & show n & " bus1=" & show b1 & " bus2=" & show b2 & " truncated=" & (if scan.truncated then "yes" else "no"))
  end